  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "id": "p-before-diving-into-fancy-operations-and-interview-tricks-let-s-just-understand-nbsp-strong-what-a-linked-list-actually-is-nbsp-strong-nbsp-from-scratch-p",
      "title": "<p>Before diving into fancy operations and interview tricks, let's just understand&nbsp;<strong>what a linked list actually is&nbsp;</strong>&nbsp;from scratch.</p>",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1-the-problem-with-arrays",
      "title": "1. The Problem With Arrays",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Say you want to store a list of student marks:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251537353-606870783.png\" alt=\"mermaid-diagram-45.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Arrays work great here but they come with a hidden constraint. When you create an array, your computer&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">reserves a fixed block of memory&nbsp;</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">for it upfront.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251539536-144240634.png\" alt=\"Big Problem Solving Pipeline-2026-03-18-181931.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></span>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">All slots sit side by side. That's what makes index access fast jump to address 100, add the index, done.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "But what happens when you want to&nbsp;<strong>insert a new mark in the middle?</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><br></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251541074-867878602.png\" alt=\"Big Problem Solving Pipeline-2026-03-18-182532.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></strong>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><br></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251542706-369375663.png\" alt=\"Big Problem Solving Pipeline-2026-03-18-182846.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"><p><br></p>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every element after the insertion point has to&nbsp;<strong>physically move</strong>&nbsp;to make room. For large lists, this gets expensive fast.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "And if the array is already full? You have to allocate a bigger one and&nbsp;<strong>copy everything over</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-enter-the-linked-list",
      "title": "2. Enter the Linked List",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A linked list takes a completely different approach. Instead of storing everything in one big reserved block, it stores each value&nbsp;<strong>independently</strong>&nbsp;wherever memory is available.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each value lives inside a small container called a&nbsp;<strong>node</strong>:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251544766-284116123.png\" alt=\"Big Problem Solving Pipeline-2026-03-18-183040.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every node holds two things:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong>data</strong>&nbsp;the actual value you want to store",
            "<strong>next</strong>&nbsp;a pointer to where the next node lives in memory"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You chain these nodes together, one pointing to the next:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251546654-874716495.png\" alt=\"mermaid-diagram-46.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The first node is called the&nbsp;<strong>head</strong>&nbsp;it's your entry point into the list. The last node points to&nbsp;<strong>null</strong>, meaning the chain ends here.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-how-memory-actually-looks",
      "title": "3. How Memory Actually Looks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Unlike arrays, the nodes are&nbsp;<strong>not sitting next to each other</strong>. They're scattered across memory, linked purely by pointers:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Array (contiguous):",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251548170-58427167.png\" alt=\"Big Problem Solving Pipeline-2026-03-18-183820.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong>Start at address 100&nbsp;</strong>Value =&nbsp;<code>88&nbsp;</code>Next = go to address&nbsp;<code>400</code>",
            "<strong>At address 400&nbsp;</strong>Value =&nbsp;<code>92&nbsp;</code>Next = go to address&nbsp;<code>250</code>",
            "<strong>At address 250&nbsp;</strong>Value =&nbsp;<code>75&nbsp;</code>Next = go to address&nbsp;<code>630</code>",
            "<strong>At address 630&nbsp;</strong>Value =&nbsp;<code>60&nbsp;</code>Next =&nbsp;<code>null</code>&nbsp;→ means&nbsp;<strong>end of list</strong>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "What keeps them connected isn't location it's those arrows (pointers).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "What a Node Looks Like in Code",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "class Node {\npublic:\n    int data;\n    Node* next;\n\n    // Constructor\n    Node(int data) {\n        this->data = data;\n        this->next = NULL;\n    }\n};"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Creating a few nodes and linking them manually:</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"></span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "Node first  = new Node(88);\nNode second = new Node(92);\nNode third  = new Node(75);\n\nfirst.next  = second;   // 88 → 92\nsecond.next = third;    // 92 → 75\n// third.next is already null"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Visually, that gives you:</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251550088-40512902.png\" alt=\"mermaid-diagram-47.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></span>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "That's it. That's a linked list.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-what-makes-it-different-from-nbsp-array",
      "title": "3. What Makes It Different From&nbsp;Array",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong>Memory:</strong><br>Array → elements stored together<br>Linked List → elements stored separately (connected by pointers)",
            "<strong>Size:</strong><br>Array → fixed size<br>Linked List → can grow anytime",
            "<strong>Access:</strong><br>Array → fast (O(1), direct index)<br>Linked List → slow (O(n), need to travel node by node)",
            "<strong>Insert/Delete:</strong><br>Array → slow (need shifting)<br>Linked List → fast (just change pointers)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Neither is inherently better they solve different problems.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Arrays are ideal when you need&nbsp;<strong>fast index-based access</strong>, while linked lists are better suited for&nbsp;<strong>frequent insertions and deletions</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-one-nbsp-thing-nbsp-to-nbsp-keep-nbsp-in-nbsp-mind",
      "title": "4. One&nbsp;Thing&nbsp;to&nbsp;Keep&nbsp;in&nbsp;Mind",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251552031-399501131.png\" alt=\"mermaid-diagram-48.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>60th Element:&nbsp;you have&nbsp;to&nbsp;walk&nbsp;all the way here</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Because nodes are stored in scattered memory and there is no indexing,&nbsp;<strong>you cannot directly access any node</strong>.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To reach a specific element (like the 4th), you must start from the head and move step by step until you get there.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5-types-of-linked-lists",
      "title": "5. Types of Linked Lists",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now that you know what a linked list is, let's look at the&nbsp;<strong>different ways nodes can be connected</strong>. Depending on how the pointers are set up, we get three different types.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "1. Singly Linked List",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is the one you already saw. Each node has exactly&nbsp;<strong>one pointer</strong>&nbsp;pointing forward to the next node. That's it.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785251553770-641877506.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785251553770-641877506.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785251553770-641877506.png",
          "alt": "Singly linked list with one forward pointer per node"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "class Node {\n    int data;\n    Node next;\n\n    Node(int data) {\n        this.data = data;\n        this.next = null;\n    }\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Where it’s used:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Used in simple traversal problems, stacks, and basic queues anywhere you only need to move forward.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "2. Doubly Linked List",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>What if you need to go backward?</strong><br>That’s where a&nbsp;<strong>doubly linked list</strong>&nbsp;comes in.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Each node has&nbsp;<strong>two pointers</strong>: one pointing to the&nbsp;<strong>next node&nbsp;</strong>one pointing to the&nbsp;<strong>previous node</strong>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This allows you to move&nbsp;<strong>both forward and backward</strong>&nbsp;easily.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785251556202-155581224.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785251556202-155581224.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785251556202-155581224.png",
          "alt": "Doubly linked list with next and prev pointers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Zooming</b> into a single node:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785251559353-413115491.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "class DNode {\n    int data;\n    DNode next;\n    DNode prev;\n\n    DNode(int data) {\n        this.data = data;\n        this.next = null;\n        this.prev = null;\n    }\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Trade-off:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each node now stores an&nbsp;<strong>extra pointer</strong>, so it uses more memory.<br>But in return, you can move&nbsp;<strong>both forward and backward</strong>, which makes many operations easier.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Where it’s used:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Browser history (back &amp; forward)",
            "Music players (previous &amp; next track)",
            "Undo/redo in text editors"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "3.Circular Linked List",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the previous types, the last node points to&nbsp;<code>null</code>, which marks the end of the list.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>But what if there is no end?</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "In a&nbsp;<strong>circular linked list</strong>, the last node (tail) points back to the&nbsp;<strong>head</strong>&nbsp;instead of&nbsp;<code>null</code>.",
            "This creates a&nbsp;<strong>loop</strong>, so the list keeps going in a circle."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "There is no true starting or ending point you can keep traversing forever.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785251561186-222898896.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785251561186-222898896.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785251561186-222898896.png",
          "alt": "Circular linked list where the tail points back to the head"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "class CNode {\n    int data;\n    CNode next;\n\n    CNode(int data) {\n        this.data = data;\n        this.next = this; // points to itself at first\n    }\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Where it’s used:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Round-robin CPU scheduling",
            "Multiplayer game turn rotation",
            "Looping slideshows or playlists"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "text": "",
          "items": [],
          "content": "{\"headers\":[\"Type\",\"Structure\",\"Characteristics\"],\"rows\":[[\"Singly\",\"[A] → [B] → [C] → null\",\"Dead end · One-way only\"],[\"Doubly\",\"null ← [A] ⇐ [B] ⇐ [C] → null\",\"Dead end, but reversible · Two-way\"],[\"Circular\",\"[A] → [B] → [C] → [A] → ...\",\"No end · Loops forever\"]]}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
